      "question_id": "Q1",
      "answer": "Currently, there is no state law addressing religious displays in public school classrooms. The measure adds a requirement that every public school classroom display a poster of the Ten Commandments.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q2",
      "answer": "Direct costs include purchasing, distributing, and mounting the physical posters in every classroom. There may also be significant legal costs for the state or local districts if the mandate is challenged in court.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q3",
      "answer": "Public school districts and taxpayers will bear the financial cost of procuring the posters and defending the policy in potential litigation.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q4",
      "answer": "School administrators and teachers will need to acquire and hang the posters to comply. Civil liberties organizations are highly likely to file lawsuits challenging the mandate under the First Amendment's Establishment Clause.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q6",
      "answer": "State lawmakers gain power over the visual environment and display content within local public school classrooms.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q9",
      "answer": "This is a statutory requirement that can be repealed by future legislation or struck down by a court if found unconstitutional.",
      "disputed": false
    }
  ],
